<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khmer Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khmer, Angkorian Empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khmer Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, dominant in mainland South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> from the ninth to the fifteenth century. It is known for monumental temple complexes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> and for an elaborate system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and canals. Accounts of the empire cover its foundation, economy, armies, governance, legacy and eventual decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khmer-empire.jpg" alt="Khmer Empire" width="298" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jembezmamy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map-of-southeast-asia_900_CE.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
